  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nA heavy rain producing MCS continues to mature across the lower\nMissouri Valley along the nose of a low-level jet early this \nmorning. The remnants of this activity are expected to translate\neastward, mostly south of our area, across central and southern\nIL around and shortly after daybreak this morning. In spite of \nthis remaining to our south, we are not out of the woods from \ngetting some showers and storms in parts of our area this \nmorning. In fact, isolated to widely scattered showers have been\nfestering over the past few hours, particularly south of I-88. \nThis activity appears to be tied to a region of isentropic \nupglide (warm air advection) centered on the 305 K surface. The \ncurrent thinking is that the modest warm air advection pattern \nwill continue to support at least widely scattered showers as \nfar north as the Chicago metro area for a few hours this \nmorning. A few lightning strikes and brief heavy downpours may \nalso accompany some of the stronger showers, but severe weather \nis not expected. \n\nThis morning shower activity looks to largely wane into this \nafternoon, though a few lingering showers cannot be ruled out, \nparticularly to the south of I-80. However, cloud cover will \npersist this afternoon, making for a mostly cloudy day. \nAfternoon temperatures will hover in the mid and upper 70s to \naround 80 inland, with slightly cooler conditions favored closer\nto the lake due to a light onshore wind component. \n\nTonight, a small (20-30%) chance for a few additional isolated \nto widely scattered showers remain in the forecast, owing to the\ncontinued modest warm air advection pattern. However, outside \nof any showers, if any breaks in the cloud cover materialize, \nthe light winds and very moist near surface conditions could set\nthe stage for fog development into early Friday morning. I \ncurrently do not have a formal mention of fog in the forecast \nfor tonight, largely due to uncertainties regarding cloud cover.\nNevertheless, it will be something to monitor. \n\nAt this point, Friday is looking to be a largely dry day across\nour area as we remain between weather systems. Instead, the \nmain focus for convection on Friday that we will be monitoring \nis a cold front, and accompanying mid-level impulse, which is \nforecast to shift eastward across the Upper Midwest during the \nday. As this front moves into our area Friday night, there is \nabout a 20% that a few non-severe showers and storms could shift\ninto our area, but the poor diurnal timing does not bode well \nfor there being a good amount of coverage. \n\nSaturday is expected to be mostly sunny and dry, but the \nweather pattern will shift quickly thereafter. As aggregate \nupper-level troughing moves east of the Great Lakes, subtropical\nridging will become dominant across much of the southern CONUS.\nThis transition will establish a seasonably strong west-\nnorthwesterly flow at mid and upper levels, extending from the \nUpper Midwest into the Great Lakes along the southern edge of a \nHudson Bay Low. This setup is likely to trigger periodic waves \nof severe thunderstorms near a quasi-stationary surface frontal \nboundary, which is projected to stall over the lower Great Lakes\nregion through early next week. While confidence in the precise\ntiming and location of specific thunderstorm clusters remains \nlow at this range, the overall pattern aligns with conceptual \nmodels for severe weather and flash flooding across the region \nfrom Sunday through at least early next week.\n\nKJB",
